  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nContinuous waves under southwest flow aloft will continue to provide periods of \nscattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms through Saturday. Atmospheric \nmoisture will continue to remain elevated through Saturday with precipitable \nwater values from 1-1.5 inches, increasing the risk for heavy rain. Looking to \nthis afternoon, hi-res models generally have showers and thunderstorms moving \ninto southeast MT through northeast WY and northern MT late \nafternoon into the evening. The environment will be most favorable\nfor a few strong thunderstorms early afternoon for far southeast \nMT. Scattered to widespread showers and embedded thunderstorms are\nexpected to linger through the overnight hours as the surface low\nmoves slowly through southeast MT. Into Friday afternoon, models \ngenerally show the next wave bringing precipitation up through \nsouth-central MT mid day, tracking east through the afternoon and \nevening. The last of the waves pushes through Saturday into the \novernight hours keeping low to moderate precipitation chances \nacross the plains with high chances for area mountains. The best \nprobabilities for at least an inch of precipitation has shifted to\nareas west of Billings, currently at a 50-70% chance Thursday-\nFriday with even higher chances for the mountains. Moderate to \nhigh chances for at least half an inch exist for the region as a \nwhole for the same time period. Use caution if recreating near any\nburn scars over the next couple days. \n\nHeights begin to build back in late weekend, bringing temperatures back to near \nnormal. With the region keeping a slight southwest flow, unsettled conditions\nwill remain in place. This will keep low to moderate chances for afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms through the period. Precipitable water values remain \nabove average (0.75-1 inch), so moderate to heavy rainfall will be likely with \nthunderstorms. \n\nHigh temperatures will be in the upper 60s to low 80s through Saturday, warming \nback into the 80s for the remainder of the period. TS",
